From: Gleb Pesin <dormancygrace@gmail.com>
Subject: The SCTP packets of one usrsctp call leave in one batched send

usrsctp hands each packet to the write callback on its own, and each became one DTLS record and one sendto: a data
channel at full rate spent a fifth of its CPU in those system calls. While a BurstScope is open on a thread,
DtlsTransport collects its records there, and the outermost scope sends them through sendBatch (juice_send_batch,
UDP_SEGMENT), every 32 at the latest. SctpTransport opens a scope around each call into usrsctp that sends: a send,
a flush, and the input of a received packet (a SACK lets the next DATA out). usrsctp's own timer thread opens none,
so a retransmission or a heartbeat still leaves at once. The burst is sent after the send lock is released.
UnsentMediaDatagrams now also counts the datagrams of such bursts that the socket did not take.
